exercises.importantHints:
## Hint 1

`HashSet<string>.Add` does the membership test and the insert in one call:
it returns `false` when the item was already there. That false IS the "seen
a second time" signal - no `Contains`, no dictionary of counts.

## Hint 2

```csharp
HashSet<string> seen = new HashSet<string>();
foreach (string id in ids)
{
    if (!seen.Add(id)) { return id; }
}
return null;
```

`CountUnique` is even shorter: construct the set straight from the sequence
and return its `Count`.

references.Ex1.cs:
// d14_sets_queues_stacks / ex1 - a set spots the repeat.
// See lessons/d14_sets_queues_stacks/README.md, "Exercise 1".

namespace CSharpDojo.D14_SetsQueuesStacks;

public static class Scanner
{
    /// <summary>
    /// The first id that appears a SECOND time, in scan order - or null when
    /// every id is unique. One pass; HashSet.Add's answer is the whole trick.
    /// </summary>
    public static string? FirstDuplicate(IEnumerable<string> ids)
    {
        HashSet<string> seen = new HashSet<string>();
        foreach (string id in ids)
        {
            if (!seen.Add(id))
            {
                return id;
            }
        }

        return null;
    }

    /// <summary>How many DISTINCT ids the scan saw.</summary>
    public static int CountUnique(IEnumerable<string> ids)
    {
        return new HashSet<string>(ids).Count;
    }
}

