exercises.importantHints:
## Hint 1

One `try`, one `catch (InvalidOperationException)`, one `finally`. Both the
try and the catch `return`, and the finally still runs after either of them -
that is what makes it the right place for the cleanup line.

Do not catch `Exception`. The gate throws a `FormatException` at you on
purpose.

## Hint 2

```csharp
try
{
    action();
    log.Add("done");
    return "ok";
}
catch (InvalidOperationException ex)
{
    log.Add("caught");
    return $"caught:{ex.Message}";
}
finally
{
    log.Add("cleanup");
}
```

The third case is the one worth pausing on: when the action throws something
this method does not catch, the exception carries on to the caller and the
`finally` still runs on the way past. Cleanup at the end of the `try` would be
skipped; cleanup in the `catch` would be skipped on success. Only `finally`
covers all three.

references.Ex1.cs:
// d22_exceptions / ex1 - try, catch, finally, in that order.
// See lessons/d22_exceptions/README.md, "Exercise 1".

namespace CSharpDojo.D22_Exceptions;

public static class Runner
{
    /// <summary>
    /// Runs the action. Returns "ok" when it completes and
    /// "caught:&lt;message&gt;" when it throws an InvalidOperationException.
    /// Any other exception escapes. Either way "cleanup" is the LAST thing
    /// added to the log - including on the way out of an escaping exception.
    /// </summary>
    public static string Attempt(Action action, List<string> log)
    {
        try
        {
            action();
            log.Add("done");
            return "ok";
        }
        catch (InvalidOperationException ex)
        {
            log.Add("caught");
            return $"caught:{ex.Message}";
        }
        finally
        {
            log.Add("cleanup");
        }
    }
}

